<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hector Berlioz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1869">1869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Louis-Hector Berlioz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hector Berlioz was a French Romantic composer, conductor and critic, born at La Côte-Saint-André, who left medical studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for music against his family's wishes. His refusal to follow traditional rules set him at odds with the conservative musical establishment of Paris, though he won the Prix de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1830. His works include the Symphonie fantastique, inspired by the actress Harriet Smithson, Harold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the Requiem, La Damnation de Faust and the operas Benvenuto Cellini, Les Troyens and Béatrice et Bénédict. Meeting only occasional success in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he gained an international reputation as a conductor, and his Treatise on Instrumentation (1844) was influential.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz.jpg" alt="Hector Berlioz" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: w:August Prinzhofer (1816–1885) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hector-Berlioz-1845.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That year Eugène Delacroix painted Liberty Leading the People, after the July Revolution, and <b>Hector Berlioz</b>’s Symphonie fantastique was first performed.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest surviving polyphonic Requiem, by Johannes Ockeghem, does not include it; the first settings to include it are by Engarandus Juvenis (1490) and Antoine Brumel (1516), and later ones by Charpentier, Delalande, Mozart, <b>Berlioz</b>, Verdi, Britten and Stravinsky.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote>Composers took up the tune as a sign of student life: <b>Berlioz</b> in La Damnation de Faust, Strauss in his &quot;Studenten-Polka&quot; for the students' ball at the Vienna Hofburg on 24 February 1862, Suppé in the overture of his operetta Flotte Bursche (1863), Liszt in a paraphrase and…</blockquote><a href="/songs/gaudeamus-igitur">Gaudeamus igitur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hector Berlioz</b> arranged it for orchestra in 1859, and in the 1880s Louis van Waefelghem arranged the tune for viola d'amore or viola and piano.</blockquote><a href="/songs/plaisir-damour">Plaisir d'amour</a></li><li><blockquote>The hymn was first sung to a Gregorian chant melody, and many composers have set it since: Lully, who was fatally injured while conducting his setting for the court of Louis XIV, Charpentier, Handel, Haydn, Mozart, <b>Berlioz</b>, Verdi, Bruckner, Dvořák and Britten among them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune for four centuries: Jehan Titelouze (1623), Marc-Antoine Charpentier (five settings), Johann Pachelbel, Nicolas de Grigny, Johann Sebastian Bach (the chorale BWV 370 and the organ preludes BWV 631 and 667), <b>Berlioz</b>, Franck, Bruckner, Gustav Mahler, who set the Latin text in Part I of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
